# Mobile web UI baselines

The product is a **mobile-first web app**. Phone viewports are the primary target; tablet/desktop are progressive enhancement.

## Tap targets and spacing

- Interactive elements (buttons, links, inputs, toggles) are **at least ~44×44 CSS px** in their hit area.
- Adjacent tappable elements get enough spacing that a thumb cannot trigger the wrong one.

## No hover-only affordances

- Anything a user must discover or trigger is reachable **without hover** (no info hidden behind `:hover` only).
- Tooltips on touch devices need a tap-to-reveal alternative.

## Forms

- Use the right input semantics: `type`, `inputMode`, `autoComplete`, `enterKeyHint`, `name`.
- Surface errors near the field, not only at the top.
- Numeric / currency inputs use `inputMode="decimal"` (or `numeric`) so the right on-screen keyboard appears.

```tsx
<input
  type="email"
  inputMode="email"
  autoComplete="email"
  enterKeyHint="next"
  name="email"
  required
/>
```

## Layout

- Default to a **single column** on small screens; use multi-column layouts only above a defined breakpoint.
- Avoid horizontal scrolling at 360 px width (smallest common phone) for primary content.
- Sticky headers / footers respect **`safe-area-inset-*`** on notched devices.

```css
.bottom-bar {
  padding-bottom: max(0.75rem, env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
}
```

## Performance posture (mobile)

- Be wary of large client bundles, unbounded lists, and synchronous waterfalls — mobile networks punish all three.
- Coordinate with `project-creative-design.mdc`: visual ambition must not balloon the critical-path payload.
